A||||15||overlord||need advice before i do this..||||1||
Z||000000||overlord||01-29-2001||11:24 AM||||i bought a jug of full synthetic oil...but now im not sure if i should switch.<BR>I did some research, and they say you should flush the enigne first, to get all mineral oil deposits out. However those deposits might of been acting as seals on various spots.<P>I havent a clue to what has been put in this engine (bought at wrecker), so should i just flush it and switch to synthetic and see what happens?  I so dont want any oil to start leaking and start a fire.<BR>Or just leave it?<P>||||reg||1
Z||000001||overlord||01-29-2001||12:00 PM||||ok now im really confused.<BR>Did some more reading on syntheic oils, and some people said they switched at over the 100,000 mile mark with no problems or leaks.<BR>But some poeple swear you shouldnt do this cuase u might develop leaks.<P>anyone??<P>||||reg||1
Z||000002||kinboyatuwo||01-29-2001||12:46 PM||||Replied before to a question like this and asked it too.  I switced over at 220,000km and have yet to have a problem.  Did the enging flush and the car has run great since.  Might be a luck  of the draw thing as to whether it will work or not!<P>sorry no difinitive answer<P>------------------<BR>"Friends don't let friends drive four doors."||||reg||4
Z||000003||TheFiero64||01-29-2001||12:51 PM||||I switched over on 3 cars all over 100K. I have had no problems and have been using synthetic for 3 years.||||reg||1
Z||000004||terryk||01-29-2001||01:31 PM||||The only problem is if your leaks are being sealed by gunk. If so, the synth will clean that out after a few oil changes and the engine will leak. If not, then no problems. <P>Terry K||||reg||1
Z||000005||rockcrawl||01-29-2001||05:33 PM||||There is more to it than just deposits sealing leaks, although that may also be part of the problem. Mineral oil has a swelling effect on almost all types of engine seals, cork and rubber(cork seals are impregnated with rubber). Synthetic oil does not cause seals to swell. When switching to synthetic, some of the seals may shrink back to their original size causing a leak. <P>------------------<BR>Jon Lagler<BR>'87 GT<BR>4.9 liter V8 Testarossa Spyder (under construction)<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/rockcrawlsig.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000006||FieroJoe||01-29-2001||05:58 PM||||What about doing the kerosene engine flush?  Does that eat away at seals and gaskets?||||reg||1
Z||000007||overlord||01-29-2001||06:28 PM||||so u think i should do this?<BR>i was gonna  do it today, but chickened out.<P>I'm startin to lean towards the "not doing it" side now..with all the leaks, seals, and whatnot stuff ive read.||||reg||1
Z||000008||88formula||01-29-2001||06:29 PM||||If your car has high mileage than you’re wasting your money. Using regular oil and changing it every 3500-4000 miles is all you need. There is a chance that you can develop oil leaks in a high mileage engine that has been run with regular oil all its life if you switch to synthetic oil. Synthetic oil is more slippery than ordinary oil and can get past your seals if they are worn and they most likely are.<P>Synthetic oil is good stuff though expensive and I think it should only be used in an engine if the engine is new. I don’t think you save any money in the long run when you use synthetic oil. Some people here will say you do because you can go longer between oil changes but I would not exceed 4000 miles no matter what they say. <P>That’s just my opinion. <P><BR>O-yeah changing your oil every 3500-4000 miles cleans the engine better than flushing with kerosene.  I don't believe flushing with kerosene is good for the engine bearings anyway.<P><BR>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88formula_logo_1.JPG">  <p>[This message has been edited by 88formula (edited 01-29-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000009||rogergarrison||01-29-2001||06:58 PM||||mine was low milage, but I switched straight from Valvoline DUralube to Castol Syntec. just filter and oil, no flush. been fine for 40K (knock on wood) I used to drain oil and flush with kerosene ages ago when i bought a used car. just ran it for a few minutes then changed it back to oil. Another thing I used to do was when it got a quart low, id add a qt of trans fluid and drive it a few days then change it. Its really got some potent solvents. ever seen how clean the inside of a auto trans is. I never had any engine probs either way.||||reg||1
Z||000010||theogre||01-29-2001||07:33 PM||||Castrol.... the one synthetic Not to use. Castrol is taking allot of heat because their version of "synthetic" is made from crude. This has only been covered in the "trade rags" not the mainstream media. I've been watching that one for awhile.<P>The major brand synthets all have aditives to prevent exactly what Rock is describing. If it is certified to current API and GL spec it will not cause trouble in any motor in decent shape. People reporting "real" problems have generally turned out to be motors that had prolems before the synthetic was installed.<P>I'll probably be putting synthetic in my car next change. mine has over 150k||||reg||1
Z||000011||overlord||01-30-2001||12:02 AM||||ok thanks guys,<BR>i dont  know exactly how many miles are on the engine but  imn assuming at least 100,000 kms.  I'll stick  with the regular i guess. See if i can exchange this sytnthetic stuff for a couple jugs o regular. I change my oil every 4000 kms (2500? miles) anyways. I just thought that the better the oil you put in then the better car would run. Didnt know oil was so complicated.||||reg||1
Z||000012||Tom Philips||01-30-2001||07:47 AM||||Save your money people... there are many articles out there that say the only thing synthetic is good for is its resistance to heat it won't solidify as fast as mineral oil as for extending oil changes remember that all the carbon and unburnt fuel is still there eating away at your engine after the 3000 mile recomended change for me i would rather change the oil more often and spend less. Just my .02||||reg||1
Z||000013||rogergarrison||01-30-2001||08:06 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Tom Philips:<BR><B>Save your money people... there are many articles out there that say the only thing synthetic is good for is its resistance to heat it won't solidify as fast as mineral oil as for extending oil changes remember that all the carbon and unburnt fuel is still there eating away at your engine after the 3000 mile recomended change for me i would rather change the oil more often and spend less. Just my .02</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>thats why i went to synthetic. I run a turbo and the extra heat cooks oil. I used Castrol because thats what I always used in the Mercedes, before synthetic was around. Sorry but I havent had a single oil related prob for years. Anything that gave up on me was usually electrical.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000014||montage||01-30-2001||09:04 AM||||One benefit of syth oil that has not been discussed is it reduces friction of the rotating parts. This would lead to higher efficiency of the engine and increase HP and torque. However, this is probably a very small increase. This is the reason snyth oil should not be used as the first oil in a new or rebuilt engine. This doesn't allow the engine to break in (or wear in) properly.||||reg||1
Z||000015||ironhedno3||01-30-2001||07:25 PM||||all syn oil is made from crude base stock..it doesnt cause leaks but will find them if they are there already..oh by the way..nothin beats mobile one..especially for high heat applications like fieros or turbo cars||||reg||1
